<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd al-Ilah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1913–1958</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd al-Ilah was regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 1939 to 1953 for his young nephew King Faisal II, and remained crown prince and a dominant influence afterwards. A member of the Hashemite family and a firm ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, he fled Rashid Ali's coup in 1941 and returned with British troops. He was killed with the king in the revolution of 14 July 1958.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-ilah.jpg" alt="Abd al-Ilah" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cecil Beaton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%27Abd_al-Ilah_of_Hejaz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
